.TH RUSTMON 1 "2026-08-12" "rustmon 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
rustmon \- read\-only Linux hardware monitor: live TUI or one\-shot text/JSON snapshot
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rustmon
.br
.B rustmon \-\-once
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.br
.B rustmon \-\-summary
.SH DESCRIPTION
With no arguments,
.B rustmon
opens a live, full\-screen terminal UI showing CPU, memory, temperature,
fan, disk, network, and GPU state, refreshed on an interval (see
.B KEY BINDINGS
below).
.PP
.B \-\-once
takes a single reading instead, prints it as aligned text or JSON, and
exits \(em no interactive loop, no rate/throughput figures (those need two
readings, which would make a supposedly one\-shot command block for an
interval), suitable for scripts and cron.
.PP
.B \-\-summary
prints at most one short line and exits, intended to run before every
shell prompt \(em see
.B SUMMARY MODE
below.
.PP
.B rustmon
is read\-only by design: it never writes to
.IR /proc " or " /sys ,
never spawns a subprocess, and never requires root. Anything it can't
read is reported as absent rather than as an error.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-1\fR, \fB\-\-once\fR
One snapshot to stdout, then exit (no TUI).
.TP
\fB\-\-summary\fR
Shell\-prompt one\-liner, then exit. See
.BR "SUMMARY MODE" .
Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-\-once ;
if both are given, the last one wins.
.TP
\fB\-f\fR, \fB\-\-format\fR \fIFORMAT\fR
Output format for
.BR \-\-once :
.I text
(default) or
.IR json .
The JSON schema is documented in the project's README.
.TP
\fB\-i\fR, \fB\-\-interval\fR \fITIME\fR
Refresh interval: a bare number of milliseconds, or a value like
.IR 500ms " or " 2s
(default: 1s, floor: 100ms).
.TP
\fB\-\-only\fR \fILIST\fR
Run only these collectors, comma\-separated (e.g.
.IR cpu,memory ).
.TP
\fB\-\-skip\fR \fILIST\fR
Never run these collectors, comma\-separated; applied after
.BR \-\-only .
.TP
\fB\-\-sysfs\-root\fR \fIPATH\fR
Root for all filesystem reads (default:
.IR / ).
.TP
\fB\-\-history\fR \fIN\fR
Samples retained for sparklines (default: 120, ceiling: 3600). Only
matters for the live TUI.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Include the collector error list in output.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print usage and exit.
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print the version and compiled\-in features, then exit.
.SH KEY BINDINGS
These apply to the live TUI only (no effect under
.BR \-\-once " or " \-\-summary ).
.TP
.B q, Esc, Ctrl\-C
Quit.
.TP
.B Tab, Shift\-Tab
Cycle panel focus.
.TP
.B 1\-7
Jump directly to a panel (CPU, memory, thermal, disk, net, GPU,
connections).
.TP
.B space
Pause/resume the refresh loop.
.TP
.B r
Reset the rate tracker \(em the next refresh drops its interval rather
than averaging across the pause.
.TP
.B ?
Toggle the help overlay.
.TP
\fB+\fR, \fB\-\fR
Adjust the refresh interval (floor: 100ms).
.PP
With the connections panel focused, connections are grouped into a
collapsible tree by real OS parent/child process relationship (each
process's
.I PPid
from
.IR /proc/<pid>/status ):
.TP
.B Up, Down
Move the row cursor.
.TP
.B x
Toggle the cursor row's checkbox. On a process\-header row this checks or
unchecks every connection in that process's entire subtree at once \(em
its own connections plus every nested subprocess's \(em so
.B Enter
can resolve a whole group without expanding it first.
.TP
.B Left, Right
Collapse or expand the cursor row's process group. Collapsing only
changes what's drawn; a bulk checkbox toggle on a collapsed row still
reaches every connection underneath it.
.TP
.B Enter
Resolve every checked row's remote IP: a domain name (a hand\-rolled
reverse\-DNS lookup) and, on builds with the
.B traceroute
feature, a route (raw ICMP, one hop per TTL). Both are cached per IP \(em
checking one connection resolves every other connection sharing that
address too \(em and never triggered automatically, only on request, for
exactly the rows you check.
.PP
Domain resolution shows
.I resolving...
while pending,
.I "no PTR record"
if the query succeeded but found nothing, or
.I failed
on a timeout or an unreachable/unconfigured resolver.
.PP
Route tracing (requires
.B CAP_NET_RAW
\(em see
.B PRIVILEGES
below) shows
.I tracing...
while pending, then each hop's address separated by
.RI \(lq " \-> " \(rq ,
with
.B *
for a hop that didn't reply in time (the standard
.BR traceroute (8)
convention), or
.I unavailable
if the raw socket couldn't be created at all (no
.BR CAP_NET_RAW ,
or the
.B traceroute
feature wasn't compiled in).
.SH PRIVILEGES
Every collector in
.B rustmon
runs fully unprivileged \(em see
.B "What needs root, and why"
in the project README. Route tracing (this section exists because of it) is
the one exception: raw ICMP sockets need the
.B CAP_NET_RAW
capability. Without it, route tracing degrades to
.I unavailable
in the connections panel rather than erroring \(em everything else in
.B rustmon
is completely unaffected. To use it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo setcap cap_net_raw+ep $(command \-v rustmon)
.fi
.RE
.PP
grants the capability to the binary itself, so
.B rustmon
never needs to run as root afterward. Do this only if you actually want
route tracing; it is entirely optional.
.SH SUMMARY MODE
.B rustmon \-\-summary
is built for a shell prompt (e.g. an oh\-my\-posh command segment): it must
run well under 100ms, so it deliberately collects only
.IR cpu ", " memory ", and " thermal
\(em the three collectors in this crate with no exposure to a slow read (no
.BR statvfs (2)
call, no
.I /proc
process\-table walk) \(em regardless of any
.B \-\-only
or
.B \-\-skip
also given.
.PP
If there is nothing worth surfacing (no sensor at warning/critical
severity, no collector error), it prints
.B nothing
and exits
.BR 1 .
Otherwise it prints one compact JSON object to stdout and exits
.BR 0 :
.PP
.RS
.nf
{"status": "warning", "headline": "2 warnings"}
.fi
.RE
.PP
.I status
is one of
.IR ok ", " warning ", or " critical .
.I headline
is short enough to sit in a colored prompt segment next to a git branch,
not a paragraph. A shell prompt integration that hides its segment on a
nonzero exit code or empty output (oh\-my\-posh's command segment does
this out of the box) needs no other configuration to appear only when
there's something to say.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Normal exit \(em the TUI was quit,
.B \-\-once
completed successfully, or
.B \-\-summary
had something to report.
.TP
.B 1
A configuration error (an unknown flag, a missing flag value, an invalid
.BR \-\-sysfs\-root ,
or an unknown collector name in
.BR \-\-only " / " \-\-skip ),
or \(em under
.B \-\-summary
specifically \(em a deliberate signal that there is nothing worth
surfacing this run, not a failure.
.SH OUTPUT
.B \-\-once \-\-format json
emits one JSON object per invocation: a fixed
.I schema
version, a UTC timestamp, and one section per collector that produced
data (a machine with no GPU has no
.I gpu
key at all, not an empty one). Every string is escaped per RFC 8259 and
sanitised of control characters/ANSI sequences before it reaches the
output. Full field\-by\-field documentation: the project's README.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Start the live TUI:
.B rustmon
.TP
One snapshot as aligned text:
.B rustmon \-\-once
.TP
One snapshot as JSON, piped to a tool that wants it:
.B rustmon \-\-once \-\-format json | jq .
.TP
Just CPU and memory, refreshed every 2 seconds:
.B rustmon \-\-only cpu,memory \-\-interval 2s
.TP
Point at a fixture tree instead of the real machine:
.B rustmon \-\-once \-\-sysfs\-root /path/to/fixture
.TP
Shell\-prompt one\-liner:
.B rustmon \-\-summary
.SH LIMITATIONS
.IP \(bu 2
Linux only.
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-\-once
never takes two readings to compute a rate \(em throughput and CPU busy%
exist only in the live TUI.
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-\-summary
only ever looks at CPU, memory, and thermal state \(em disk, net, and GPU
are never part of its headline, by design (see
.BR "SUMMARY MODE" ).
.IP \(bu 2
NVIDIA GPU metrics are not supported (no NVIDIA hardware to target;
AMD/Intel GPU support is unaffected).
.IP \(bu 2
A handful of hwmon sensors (some power/voltage sensors on server\-class
boards) are root\-only under some kernel configurations. Unprivileged is
still the recommended way to run
.BR rustmon ;
that one sensor is reported as absent rather than as an error, and every
other sensor on the same chip is unaffected.
.IP \(bu 2
Route tracing is IPv4 only \(em an IPv6 remote connection never gets a
route (its checkbox still resolves a domain name normally). Needs
.B CAP_NET_RAW
(see
.BR PRIVILEGES ),
and only exists in builds with the
.B traceroute
Cargo feature (on by default).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR top (1),
.BR free (1),
.BR sensors (1),
.BR ss (1),
.BR df (1),
.BR traceroute (8)
.SH AUTHOR
See the project repository for source, design notes, and the build log:
